= Public good =

== Overview ==
'''Public good''' is a good whose use is substantially non-rival and from which exclusion is difficult or costly under the relevant technological and institutional conditions.

Non-rivalry means one person's use does not substantially reduce the amount available to others. Non-excludability means it is difficult or costly to prevent non-payers or non-members from benefiting.

Public goods need not be produced by governments, and government-produced goods need not be public goods in the economic sense. The classification concerns properties of consumption and exclusion, not ownership alone.

Non-excludability can create free-rider incentives because a person may benefit without contributing. Actual provision can come from governments, communities, firms, philanthropy, norms, bundled services, or other institutions.

== Boundaries ==
* public good ≠ government-provided good
* public good ≠ commons/common-pool resource
* non-rivalry ≠ unlimited capacity under every condition

